  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a spin-driven magnetization switching mechanism in a layered magnetic material. On the left, two stacked magnetic sheets contain Mn atoms and interlayer species labeled X. Red and blue arrows indicate opposite spin directions in the two layers, while a horizontal field \\(H_s\\) and the net spin \\(S\\) are shown reversing between the upper and lower schematics. A yellow arrow labeled \\(J\\) suggests interlayer exchange coupling, and the diagrams imply that changing the spin configuration under the applied field flips the overall magnetic state.\n\nAt the upper right, a hexagonal arrangement of canted spin-like cones is drawn with two field components, \\(H_s\\) and \\(H_x\\), indicating that the response depends on both an in-plane switching field and an out-of-plane or transverse field. A nearby vector sketch connects local spin rotations to the resulting magnetization \\(M\\).\n\nThe table at the bottom right compares combinations of spin direction \\(S\\), field \\(H_x\\), and initial magnetization \\(M\\), then shows intermediate and final \\(M\\) directions and whether switching occurs. Overall, the figure conveys that magnetization reversal is selective: only certain alignments of spin and field lead to successful switching, while others leave the magnetic state unchanged.",
